1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,485
This is a walkthrough of the BigFunnel
posting list table monitor

2
00:00:03,485 --> 00:00:05,959
version one point fourteen point zero

3
00:00:05,959 --> 00:00:10,922
on a live Exchange Server Subscription
Edition database availability group.

4
00:00:10,922 --> 00:00:14,985
Subscription Edition and Exchange 2019 are
code equivalent here

5
00:00:14,985 --> 00:00:18,292
so everything in this walkthrough applies to
both.

6
00:00:18,292 --> 00:00:22,563
What you are about to see is real output
from two lab servers.

7
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:26,209
The screens were captured on version one
point eleven point zero

8
00:00:26,209 --> 00:00:28,227
so that is the version their banners show

9
00:00:28,227 --> 00:00:31,834
the only thing that looks different on
fourteen is the completion line

10
00:00:31,834 --> 00:00:35,415
which now also breaks the time down by
phase.

11
00:00:35,415 --> 00:00:39,008
We will start it from an ordinary shell and
watch it elevate itself

12
00:00:39,008 --> 00:00:40,519
read the report it prints

13
00:00:40,519 --> 00:00:42,042
watch it find something

14
00:00:42,042 --> 00:00:46,612
see why it stays quiet on an estate too
small to judge, and then schedule it

15
00:00:46,612 --> 00:00:48,813
including the one mistake that makes a
scheduled

16
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:52,392
monitor look healthy while it has never run
at all.

17
00:00:54,742 --> 00:00:57,524
This is the BigFunnel posting list table
monitor

18
00:00:57,524 --> 00:01:00,769
version one point fourteen point zero.

19
00:01:00,769 --> 00:01:04,063
It is a single read-only PowerShell script.

20
00:01:04,063 --> 00:01:08,062
Before you run it anywhere, verify the copy
you were given.

21
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:09,998
Hash it.

22
00:01:09,998 --> 00:01:11,887
The script is not code-signed

23
00:01:11,887 --> 00:01:13,840
so the hash is the only thing that tells you

24
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:16,939
the file you have is the file that was
tested.

25
00:01:16,939 --> 00:01:18,983
Check the line and byte counts too

26
00:01:18,983 --> 00:01:21,587
they catch a copy that was truncated in
transit

27
00:01:21,587 --> 00:01:23,618
which a hash tells you about only after you

28
00:01:23,618 --> 00:01:27,012
already have the right one to compare
against.

29
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:32,293
It calls three Exchange cmdlets, all of them
read-only: Get-Exchange-Server

30
00:01:32,293 --> 00:01:36,422
Get-Mailbox-Database, and
Get-Mailbox-Statistics.

31
00:01:36,422 --> 00:01:39,925
It does not move a mailbox, fail over a
database

32
00:01:39,925 --> 00:01:42,777
or change a single Exchange setting.

33
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:47,764
By default the only thing it writes is files
under the output path you give it.

34
00:01:47,764 --> 00:01:50,706
Two switches add a destination outside that
path

35
00:01:50,706 --> 00:01:53,519
dash Emit To writes to the Application event
log

36
00:01:53,519 --> 00:01:56,579
and dash Register Scheduled Task creates a
task

37
00:01:56,579 --> 00:01:59,738
and neither happens unless you ask for it.

38
00:02:02,688 --> 00:02:06,212
Start it from an ordinary shell - not an
elevated one

39
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:08,754
and it deals with that itself.

40
00:02:08,754 --> 00:02:12,152
It rebuilds its own command line from the
parameters you passed

41
00:02:12,152 --> 00:02:16,512
relaunches under Run As, and asks you to
approve the prompt.

42
00:02:16,862 --> 00:02:19,672
Here is the detail that matters.

43
00:02:19,672 --> 00:02:23,657
An elevated process cannot attach to the
console of the one that launched it

44
00:02:23,657 --> 00:02:25,870
so the relaunch gets a window of its own and

45
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:29,191
Windows closes it the instant the run ends.

46
00:02:29,191 --> 00:02:32,810
Without help you would approve a prompt,
watch a console flash past

47
00:02:32,810 --> 00:02:36,040
and be left with a pointer to a log file.

48
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:39,751
Instead the child writes each line to a
relay file as it prints it

49
00:02:39,751 --> 00:02:42,326
and the parent replays it here.

50
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:45,393
The whole report arrives, colours and all

51
00:02:45,393 --> 00:02:48,299
in the window you were already looking at.

52
00:02:48,299 --> 00:02:54,158
One critical and one warning, each named, on
its database, with its size.

53
00:02:54,158 --> 00:02:56,776
It is written line by line rather than
buffered

54
00:02:56,776 --> 00:03:01,276
so a child that dies halfway still relays
what it managed to say.

55
00:03:01,626 --> 00:03:07,978
And then it exits with the child's code, not
its own. Note what that code is.

56
00:03:07,978 --> 00:03:12,210
Zero - on a run that just found a critical
mailbox.

57
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:17,106
Codes one, five and six are gated behind
dash Exit Non Zero On Alert

58
00:03:17,106 --> 00:03:19,020
so that adding this monitor to an existing

59
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:22,679
scheduler cannot start failing tasks on day
one.

60
00:03:22,679 --> 00:03:25,179
Rather than leave that contradiction on
screen

61
00:03:25,179 --> 00:03:28,338
the report says so on the line underneath.

62
00:03:31,288 --> 00:03:33,836
Now a first run on a DAG member

63
00:03:33,836 --> 00:03:38,005
from a shell that is already elevated, so
there is no prompt.

64
00:03:38,005 --> 00:03:43,265
Scope Local discovers the databases whose
active copy is mounted on this node.

65
00:03:43,265 --> 00:03:45,921
You do not need dash Verbose: since version

66
00:03:45,921 --> 00:03:48,578
one point eight the report is printed every
run

67
00:03:48,578 --> 00:03:53,392
and Verbose only adds the individual rows
the terse report leaves out.

68
00:03:53,742 --> 00:03:57,060
It opens its own Exchange runspace.

69
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:59,013
That is what makes it schedulable

70
00:03:59,013 --> 00:04:03,063
it does not need to be launched from the
Exchange Management Shell.

71
00:04:03,063 --> 00:04:06,357
Two databases here, fifty mailboxes between
them

72
00:04:06,357 --> 00:04:08,284
and it names each one with its count rather

73
00:04:08,284 --> 00:04:11,358
than making you read that back out of a log.

74
00:04:11,708 --> 00:04:15,586
Result OK, in four point eight seconds.

75
00:04:15,586 --> 00:04:21,459
Nothing critical, nothing warning, nothing
emerging, no database failed.

76
00:04:21,459 --> 00:04:26,719
And the line under the counts is the one to
read first: Counter, Confirmed.

77
00:04:26,719 --> 00:04:29,310
That says the script proved to itself that
the metric it

78
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:33,068
depends on is actually being populated on
this estate.

79
00:04:33,418 --> 00:04:36,254
Then the honest first-run message.

80
00:04:36,254 --> 00:04:38,976
There is no previous run to compare against

81
00:04:38,976 --> 00:04:42,335
so there are no rates and no projected
dates.

82
00:04:42,335 --> 00:04:48,416
That is not an error. And notice the last
line: two counters are in use.

83
00:04:48,416 --> 00:04:51,919
Three mailboxes are dated on the posting
list table itself

84
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:55,369
the other twenty are ranked only, on index
payload bytes

85
00:04:55,369 --> 00:04:58,786
because the thresholds do not apply to that
counter.

86
00:05:01,736 --> 00:05:04,468
A run leaves four files.

87
00:05:04,468 --> 00:05:07,385
A timestamped C S V and log for history

88
00:05:07,385 --> 00:05:10,718
latest dot C S V for whatever you point a
report at

89
00:05:10,718 --> 00:05:15,896
and latest dash summary dot JSON, which is
the one you wire into monitoring.

90
00:05:16,246 --> 00:05:20,280
The C S V is where the per-mailbox detail
lives.

91
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:24,226
Filter it to the rows that carry a projected
date, sort by that date

92
00:05:24,226 --> 00:05:27,806
and you have your work queue in the order it
needs doing.

93
00:05:27,806 --> 00:05:32,950
Note the trend metric column - it tells you
which counter each row was ranked on.

94
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:38,103
Here is the summary file, trimmed to the
fields a consumer branches on.

95
00:05:38,103 --> 00:05:41,293
Status, the counter validation, the counts

96
00:05:41,293 --> 00:05:45,056
whether the run was elevated, and the exit
code.

97
00:05:45,056 --> 00:05:48,506
Alert on Status not in OK or Metric
Inconclusive

98
00:05:48,506 --> 00:05:51,900
and read the counts beside it for what was
found.

99
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:58,133
And when the monitor names a mailbox, this
is where you end up: the mailbox itself.

100
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:00,438
Two point seven gigabytes of content

101
00:06:00,438 --> 00:06:04,787
and a posting list table of seven hundred
and twenty-one megabytes.

102
00:06:04,787 --> 00:06:07,391
That is the figure the whole script is built
around

103
00:06:07,391 --> 00:06:10,610
read straight from Get-Mailbox-Statistics.

104
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:18,152
The defaults of one point seven and two
gigabytes are a recommended starting point

105
00:06:18,152 --> 00:06:19,782
not a law.

106
00:06:19,782 --> 00:06:24,274
After a fortnight you tune them to what your
estate actually looks like.

107
00:06:24,274 --> 00:06:28,245
On this lab the largest mailboxes sit around
seven hundred megabytes

108
00:06:28,245 --> 00:06:30,420
so here the thresholds are set to match

109
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:34,152
and this time with dash Exit Non Zero On
Alert.

110
00:06:34,502 --> 00:06:36,531
Result Alert.

111
00:06:36,531 --> 00:06:39,708
One critical, one warning, ranked worst
first

112
00:06:39,708 --> 00:06:44,474
and each one carrying its own growth rate on
the same line as the finding.

113
00:06:44,474 --> 00:06:49,721
Bfseed03 is over the line and gaining a
sixth of a gigabyte a day.

114
00:06:49,721 --> 00:06:54,552
Bfseed01 is over the warning line and not
growing at all.

115
00:06:54,552 --> 00:06:56,674
Those are two different pieces of work

116
00:06:56,674 --> 00:07:00,038
and you can tell them apart without opening
anything.

117
00:07:00,388 --> 00:07:05,308
Now raise the thresholds slightly, so
nothing is over the line today.

118
00:07:05,308 --> 00:07:08,654
The same mailbox comes back as Emerging:
still normal

119
00:07:08,654 --> 00:07:13,264
still under warning, but projected to cross
critical in two days.

120
00:07:13,264 --> 00:07:15,790
The exit code is six.

121
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:17,391
One means work today

122
00:07:17,391 --> 00:07:22,276
six means work before the weekend, and a run
cannot return both.

123
00:07:22,626 --> 00:07:25,202
Route the codes by meaning.

124
00:07:25,202 --> 00:07:30,957
One and six are findings about mailboxes -
send those to whoever owns the estate.

125
00:07:30,957 --> 00:07:36,087
Two, three, four and five are operational:
the monitor itself had a problem

126
00:07:36,087 --> 00:07:38,444
and that is a different queue.

127
00:07:38,444 --> 00:07:41,582
Status is ordered the same way as the exit
codes

128
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:45,906
so the two never disagree about which
condition a run is reporting.

129
00:07:48,856 --> 00:07:53,437
The same command, unchanged, on a different
DAG member.

130
00:07:53,437 --> 00:07:57,109
This one has a single mounted database and
five mailboxes

131
00:07:57,109 --> 00:07:59,344
and none of them is large.

132
00:07:59,694 --> 00:08:03,205
Nothing in scope has ever allocated a
posting list table

133
00:08:03,205 --> 00:08:06,958
so there is no posting list data to rank on
at all.

134
00:08:06,958 --> 00:08:09,445
It falls back to index payload bytes and
says

135
00:08:09,445 --> 00:08:12,426
exactly what that costs you: the ranking is
real

136
00:08:12,426 --> 00:08:14,249
but no projected dates are given

137
00:08:14,249 --> 00:08:17,694
because the thresholds do not apply to that
counter.

138
00:08:18,044 --> 00:08:22,912
And here is the reasoning in full, printed
rather than buried in a log.

139
00:08:22,912 --> 00:08:25,190
Two mailboxes read zero bytes

140
00:08:25,190 --> 00:08:29,123
and both are below the bar at which this
build would allocate a table at all

141
00:08:29,123 --> 00:08:32,834
so zero is the correct reading, not a fault.

142
00:08:32,834 --> 00:08:35,190
But read the second half.

143
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:37,391
No threshold in this run was tested

144
00:08:37,391 --> 00:08:41,948
because nothing in scope is large enough to
show whether the counter works.

145
00:08:42,298 --> 00:08:44,638
So it reports Metric Inconclusive

146
00:08:44,638 --> 00:08:48,559
the counter reads Inconclusive, and it exits
zero.

147
00:08:48,559 --> 00:08:51,814
A small estate does not get a false alarm.

148
00:08:51,814 --> 00:08:55,460
Inconclusive means it could not prove the
counter works either way

149
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:58,871
which is a different thing from proving it
is broken.

150
00:08:58,871 --> 00:09:04,882
That second case is Metric Unavailable, exit
five, and that one you do alert on.

151
00:09:07,832 --> 00:09:12,687
Now the part that catches people out.
Scheduling it.

152
00:09:12,687 --> 00:09:16,880
Here is the registration, and it looks
completely ordinary.

153
00:09:17,230 --> 00:09:25,990
It registers. No error. No warning. Nothing
at all tells you anything is wrong.

154
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:33,226
Start it, wait, and look at what the
scheduler reports. Logon type Interactive.

155
00:09:33,226 --> 00:09:37,093
A last run time of November the thirtieth,
nineteen ninety-nine

156
00:09:37,093 --> 00:09:39,463
which is the never-ran sentinel.

157
00:09:39,463 --> 00:09:42,419
And task result two six seven zero one one

158
00:09:42,419 --> 00:09:45,372
which is scheduler-speak for has not run.

159
00:09:45,722 --> 00:09:47,751
It never ran.

160
00:09:47,751 --> 00:09:54,457
And the output directory proves it - no log,
no C S V, no summary file.

161
00:09:54,457 --> 00:09:58,819
Every single place you would normally look
for a fault is simply empty.

162
00:09:58,819 --> 00:10:02,387
This is the worst kind of failure: a monitor
you believe is running

163
00:10:02,387 --> 00:10:04,652
that has never run once.

164
00:10:04,652 --> 00:10:07,517
The cause is one missing parameter.

165
00:10:07,517 --> 00:10:12,452
With dash User and no dash Password, the
task gets logon type Interactive

166
00:10:12,452 --> 00:10:16,019
which means run only when this user is
logged on.

167
00:10:16,019 --> 00:10:18,602
A service account never is.

168
00:10:21,552 --> 00:10:24,532
The fix is to supply the password.

169
00:10:24,532 --> 00:10:27,188
Prompt for it rather than writing it into
the script

170
00:10:27,188 --> 00:10:31,159
so it reaches neither source control nor
your shell history.

171
00:10:31,159 --> 00:10:34,232
A group managed service account will not
work here either

172
00:10:34,232 --> 00:10:38,112
it has no outbound network credential to
give the task.

173
00:10:38,462 --> 00:10:43,017
Never trust the registration. Verify it.

174
00:10:43,017 --> 00:10:47,210
Logon type now reads Password, there is a
real last run time

175
00:10:47,210 --> 00:10:49,766
and the task result is zero.

176
00:10:50,116 --> 00:10:53,864
And this time the output directory has
something in it.

177
00:10:53,864 --> 00:10:59,620
Four files, the same four a run by hand
produces, with a real timestamp on them.

178
00:10:59,970 --> 00:11:04,695
Three commands prove it took, and they are
worth keeping as a checklist.

179
00:11:04,695 --> 00:11:10,372
Logon type reads Password. The scheduler's
last result is zero.

180
00:11:10,372 --> 00:11:12,833
And the summary file agrees with the
scheduler

181
00:11:12,833 --> 00:11:18,366
same version, completed true, status OK,
exit code zero.

182
00:11:18,366 --> 00:11:21,452
If the file says one thing and the scheduler
says another

183
00:11:21,452 --> 00:11:25,890
something in your chain is using dash
Command instead of dash File.

184
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:31,403
To put this into production.

185
00:11:31,403 --> 00:11:37,783
Day zero: verify the hash, copy it to one
DAG member, run it by hand.

186
00:11:37,783 --> 00:11:43,213
Day one: run it again twenty-four hours
later, and you have your first rates.

187
00:11:43,213 --> 00:11:47,262
Day two: register the task on every member,
with dash File

188
00:11:47,262 --> 00:11:51,544
dash Password, and dash Exit Non Zero On
Alert.

189
00:11:51,894 --> 00:11:57,191
Day three: wire latest dash summary dot JSON
into your monitoring.

190
00:11:57,191 --> 00:12:03,702
Alert on Status, and route one and six apart
from two, three, four and five.

191
00:12:03,702 --> 00:12:04,848
And at week two

192
00:12:04,848 --> 00:12:09,582
review the rates and tune the warning and
critical thresholds to your own data.

193
00:12:09,932 --> 00:12:14,459
And the one thing to take away from all of
this: dash Password is not optional

194
00:12:14,459 --> 00:12:18,328
and a task that says Ready is not a task
that runs.

195
00:12:18,328 --> 00:12:21,980
Verify the logon type on every member you
register.

196
00:12:22,930 --> 00:12:25,168
That is the walkthrough.

197
00:12:25,168 --> 00:12:30,155
Verify the hash, run it by hand twice,
schedule it with a password on every member

198
00:12:30,155 --> 00:12:33,562
and alert on Status and the exit code
together.

